1
00:00:03,550 --> 00:00:07,075
Your RAG system just gave a
confident, wrong answer.

2
00:00:07,375 --> 00:00:11,775
The tempting fix is to tweak the prompt,
or to switch to a bigger model.

3
00:00:12,383 --> 00:00:15,233
Usually, that's the wrong place to start.

4
00:00:15,533 --> 00:00:20,178
In this video, we'll walk through a method for
finding where the answer actually

5
00:00:20,178 --> 00:00:22,733
went wrong, and fixing it so
it stays fixed.

6
00:00:23,750 --> 00:00:25,550
Here's the core idea.

7
00:00:25,850 --> 00:00:29,300
Wrong answers are a symptom,
not a diagnosis.

8
00:00:29,917 --> 00:00:35,742
A RAG answer passes through many stages:
parsing, chunking, indexing, retrieval,

9
00:00:35,742 --> 00:00:37,417
ranking and generation.

10
00:00:37,717 --> 00:00:41,592
So the first job is to find which
stage each failure came from.

11
00:00:42,617 --> 00:00:44,692
First, what does wrong mean?

12
00:00:44,992 --> 00:00:51,278
Factually incorrect, incomplete, outdated, a
hallucinated citation, or an unwanted

13
00:00:51,278 --> 00:00:51,892
refusal.

14
00:00:52,192 --> 00:00:54,767
Each one points to a different stage.

15
00:00:55,383 --> 00:00:57,458
Ask what the base rate is.

16
00:00:57,758 --> 00:01:02,833
Three anecdotes and 8% of traffic call
for very different responses.

17
00:01:03,450 --> 00:01:06,075
Second, did something change recently?

18
00:01:06,375 --> 00:01:12,024
A new embedding model, chunker, index rebuild,
prompt, model version, or corpus

19
00:01:12,024 --> 00:01:12,525
update.

20
00:01:12,825 --> 00:01:18,329
Sudden regressions are almost always a deploy
or a data change, so diff the change

21
00:01:18,329 --> 00:01:19,000
log first.

22
00:01:19,617 --> 00:01:22,392
Third, is the failure concentrated?

23
00:01:22,692 --> 00:01:28,217
Look for clusters by query type, tenant,
language, document type, or time range.

24
00:01:28,517 --> 00:01:34,386
A concentrated failure usually has a single
cause, like one broken parser or one

25
00:01:34,386 --> 00:01:35,267
stale shard.

26
00:01:36,283 --> 00:01:37,808
Step one.

27
00:01:38,108 --> 00:01:42,008
Pull 50-200 failing examples
from your logs.

28
00:01:42,617 --> 00:01:46,092
Take them from thumbs-down
ratings and escalations.

29
00:01:46,717 --> 00:01:51,467
And add a random sample of traffic,
graded by an LLM judge.

30
00:01:51,767 --> 00:01:56,921
That sample matters, because explicit complaints
are biased toward certain kinds of

31
00:01:56,921 --> 00:01:57,542
questions.

32
00:01:58,550 --> 00:02:03,175
For every request, the system has to log
enough to replay it exactly.

33
00:02:03,475 --> 00:02:05,950
The original and rewritten query.

34
00:02:06,250 --> 00:02:10,150
The retrieved chunk IDs, with
scores at every stage.

35
00:02:10,450 --> 00:02:12,550
The final prompt, as sent.

36
00:02:12,850 --> 00:02:15,225
The model and prompt versions.

37
00:02:15,525 --> 00:02:17,225
And the output.

38
00:02:17,850 --> 00:02:23,025
If you can't replay a request exactly,
fixing observability is step zero.

39
00:02:23,325 --> 00:02:25,725
Every later step depends on it.

40
00:02:26,750 --> 00:02:28,875
Step two is the core move.

41
00:02:29,175 --> 00:02:34,364
For each failing case, have a subject-matter
expert find the gold evidence: the

42
00:02:34,364 --> 00:02:36,925
passage that contains the right answer.

43
00:02:37,550 --> 00:02:42,803
Then walk that case downstream, and find the
first stage where the gold evidence

44
00:02:42,803 --> 00:02:43,525
disappears.

45
00:02:43,825 --> 00:02:46,100
That stage is the root cause.

46
00:02:47,117 --> 00:02:50,242
Is the gold evidence in the
raw corpus at all?

47
00:02:50,542 --> 00:02:52,817
If not, it's a content gap.

48
00:02:53,417 --> 00:02:59,146
Is it intact after parsing, or did a table
get flattened, or OCR turn it into

49
00:02:59,146 --> 00:02:59,742
garbage?

50
00:03:00,350 --> 00:03:05,250
Is it whole within one chunk, or split away
from the context that explains it?

51
00:03:05,850 --> 00:03:10,425
Is the chunk in the index, current, with the
right permissions and metadata?

52
00:03:11,050 --> 00:03:14,300
Is it in the top 200 retrieval candidates?

53
00:03:14,917 --> 00:03:19,317
Did it survive ranking, into the
final context the model sees?

54
00:03:19,917 --> 00:03:23,492
If the gold evidence reached the
model, check the label.

55
00:03:23,792 --> 00:03:26,792
Is the expected answer actually valid?

56
00:03:27,417 --> 00:03:32,867
If it is, and the answer is still wrong, only
then is it a generation failure.

57
00:03:33,883 --> 00:03:36,758
Each case lands in exactly one bucket.

58
00:03:37,058 --> 00:03:42,480
Count them, because the histogram, not the most
memorable anecdote, decides what to

59
00:03:42,480 --> 00:03:43,133
fix first.

60
00:03:43,433 --> 00:03:47,508
Here's an illustrative distribution,
not a published benchmark.

61
00:03:48,117 --> 00:03:52,242
Retrieval or ranking, 30-40% of failures.

62
00:03:52,850 --> 00:03:55,800
Generation, 20-30%.

63
00:03:56,417 --> 00:03:59,692
Corpus or parsing, 10-20%.

64
00:04:00,317 --> 00:04:04,692
And ambiguous questions or
bad labels, 10-20%.

65
00:04:04,992 --> 00:04:07,192
Don't skip that last bucket.

66
00:04:07,492 --> 00:04:12,367
You can't fix what was never broken, and
leaving it in inflates your error rate.

67
00:04:12,667 --> 00:04:16,217
Your numbers will differ, which is
the point of measuring.

68
00:04:17,217 --> 00:04:19,267
Budget the time honestly.

69
00:04:19,567 --> 00:04:23,442
Manual attribution takes about
2-5 minutes per case.

70
00:04:24,050 --> 00:04:26,125
Across 200 cases,

71
00:04:26,750 --> 00:04:30,075
that's 7-17 hours of expert time.

72
00:04:30,375 --> 00:04:35,844
So build the taxonomy by hand once, then scale
it with an LLM judge that you've

73
00:04:35,844 --> 00:04:38,475
calibrated against those human labels.

74
00:04:39,483 --> 00:04:43,058
Step three: targeted
diagnostics for each bucket.

75
00:04:43,358 --> 00:04:45,483
Start with retrieval misses.

76
00:04:45,783 --> 00:04:52,111
Compare your production retriever against BM25
keyword search, and against exact,

77
00:04:52,111 --> 00:04:53,908
brute-force kNN search.

78
00:04:54,517 --> 00:04:59,927
If exact search finds the gold chunk but your
approximate index doesn't, the index

79
00:04:59,927 --> 00:05:00,917
is the problem.

80
00:05:01,217 --> 00:05:07,385
Search parameters like efSearch or nprobe are
set too low, or recall collapsed under

81
00:05:07,385 --> 00:05:09,442
a selective metadata filter.

82
00:05:10,050 --> 00:05:13,750
If neither finds it, the problem
is the representation.

83
00:05:14,050 --> 00:05:18,600
Acronyms, IDs and part numbers favor BM25.

84
00:05:18,900 --> 00:05:24,200
A question phrased very differently from the
document calls for query rewriting, or

85
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:28,350
HyDE, which embeds a hypothetical
answer instead of the question.

86
00:05:29,350 --> 00:05:31,550
Ranking misses look like this.

87
00:05:31,850 --> 00:05:37,396
The gold chunk was retrieved, but it's at rank
40, and the context only takes the

88
00:05:37,396 --> 00:05:37,875
top 10.

89
00:05:38,483 --> 00:05:43,910
Add or tune a cross-encoder reranker, and make
sure it sees each chunk together with

90
00:05:43,910 --> 00:05:45,783
its title and section header.

91
00:05:46,783 --> 00:05:50,008
For generation failures, run an ablation.

92
00:05:50,308 --> 00:05:53,733
Give the model only the gold
chunk, and nothing else.

93
00:05:54,350 --> 00:05:59,925
If it now answers correctly, the problem was
distraction from irrelevant context.

94
00:06:00,225 --> 00:06:04,325
Reduce k, rerank harder, and put
the best evidence first.

95
00:06:04,950 --> 00:06:09,475
If it still fails, the problem is
reasoning or instruction following.

96
00:06:09,775 --> 00:06:15,115
Require the model to quote the evidence before
answering, use a stronger model, or

97
00:06:15,115 --> 00:06:17,525
break multi-hop questions into steps.

98
00:06:18,550 --> 00:06:20,275
Two more buckets.

99
00:06:20,575 --> 00:06:26,345
If the model picked an old version of a policy,
add date metadata, recency boosting,

100
00:06:26,345 --> 00:06:28,200
and near-duplicate removal.

101
00:06:28,500 --> 00:06:32,800
Tell the model to prefer the newest
source, and to flag conflicts.

102
00:06:33,417 --> 00:06:39,292
For hallucinated citations, check that every
cited ID was actually in the context.

103
00:06:39,592 --> 00:06:46,023
Then verify each claim against its cited
passage, with an NLI model or an LLM judge,

104
00:06:46,023 --> 00:06:48,167
before returning the answer.

105
00:06:49,183 --> 00:06:50,733
Step four.

106
00:06:51,033 --> 00:06:56,130
Fix the largest bucket first, then re-run
the whole golden set, not just the

107
00:06:56,130 --> 00:06:56,733
failures.

108
00:06:57,033 --> 00:07:02,258
Changes like a smaller k or a new chunker
routinely break other slices.

109
00:07:02,883 --> 00:07:08,473
Report metrics per stage: retrieval recall at
k, context precision, faithfulness,

110
00:07:08,473 --> 00:07:11,233
answer correctness, and abstention rate.

111
00:07:11,850 --> 00:07:17,305
Add every root-caused failure to a regression
suite, with its stage label, and gate

112
00:07:17,305 --> 00:07:18,225
deploys on it.

113
00:07:18,850 --> 00:07:23,822
And monitor the signals that drift before
users complain: retrieval score

114
00:07:23,822 --> 00:07:29,544
distributions, the empty-result rate, the
abstention rate, and answers with no valid

115
00:07:29,544 --> 00:07:30,225
citations.

116
00:07:31,250 --> 00:07:34,075
Now, how do you know a fix
actually worked?

117
00:07:34,375 --> 00:07:36,600
Small eval sets are noisy.

118
00:07:36,900 --> 00:07:42,325
Compare old and new systems on the same
queries, and report confidence intervals.

119
00:07:42,950 --> 00:07:44,875
Here's a worked example.

120
00:07:45,175 --> 00:07:49,275
300 queries, with accuracy around 70%.

121
00:07:49,883 --> 00:07:55,058
The standard error of that single
accuracy number is about 2.6 points.

122
00:07:55,683 --> 00:07:57,333
Pairing helps.

123
00:07:57,633 --> 00:08:03,467
But if 10% of queries flip between the two
systems, the 95% interval on the

124
00:08:03,467 --> 00:08:07,433
difference is still about plus
or minus 3.6 points.

125
00:08:07,733 --> 00:08:11,958
So a 2-point change on 300
queries is usually noise.

126
00:08:12,983 --> 00:08:17,883
If you use an LLM judge, calibrate it
against human labels first.

127
00:08:18,183 --> 00:08:23,658
Aim for at least 85% agreement, and
re-check after every prompt change.

128
00:08:23,958 --> 00:08:29,818
Optimizing against an uncalibrated judge is a
Goodhart trap: the metric goes up, and

129
00:08:29,818 --> 00:08:31,283
real quality doesn't.

130
00:08:31,883 --> 00:08:35,683
And keep faithfulness and
correctness as separate metrics.

131
00:08:35,983 --> 00:08:40,358
An answer that's faithful to an
outdated document is still wrong.

132
00:08:41,383 --> 00:08:43,183
Here are the tradeoffs.

133
00:08:43,483 --> 00:08:48,458
Manual attribution by experts gives the
best root cause, but it's slow.

134
00:08:48,758 --> 00:08:52,683
Use it for the first pass, and for
any new kind of failure.

135
00:08:53,283 --> 00:08:59,589
An LLM judge covers thousands of cases cheaply,
once the taxonomy is stable and the

136
00:08:59,589 --> 00:09:01,108
judge is calibrated.

137
00:09:01,717 --> 00:09:05,692
A bigger model is quick, and fixes
some reasoning failures.

138
00:09:05,992 --> 00:09:10,042
But it doesn't fix retrieval, and it
adds cost and latency.

139
00:09:10,650 --> 00:09:16,221
Tuning retrieval, with hybrid search, reranking
or a fine-tuned embedder, fixes the

140
00:09:16,221 --> 00:09:19,375
most common bucket, at the
cost of re-indexing.

141
00:09:20,383 --> 00:09:22,283
Which leads to the rule.

142
00:09:22,583 --> 00:09:27,461
Don't touch the prompt or the model until you
know the gold evidence made it into

143
00:09:27,461 --> 00:09:28,183
the context.

144
00:09:28,783 --> 00:09:31,408
Many teams do it the other way around.

145
00:09:32,417 --> 00:09:34,742
Finally, the common mistakes.

146
00:09:35,042 --> 00:09:40,213
First: tweaking prompts or upgrading the model
before checking whether retrieval

147
00:09:40,213 --> 00:09:41,442
found the evidence.

148
00:09:42,050 --> 00:09:47,964
Second: debugging from three anecdotes, with no
failure set, no traces and no base

149
00:09:47,964 --> 00:09:48,325
rate.

150
00:09:48,625 --> 00:09:52,425
You end up fixing the memorable
case, not the common one.

151
00:09:53,050 --> 00:09:58,700
Third: re-testing only the failing cases, so
regressions elsewhere ship unnoticed.

152
00:09:59,317 --> 00:10:01,992
And fourth: missing a broken index.

153
00:10:02,292 --> 00:10:07,131
A collapse in filtered search recall, or a
version mismatch between the query

154
00:10:07,131 --> 00:10:11,467
encoder and the document encoder, both
look like the model got worse.

155
00:10:12,483 --> 00:10:14,158
So, to recap.

156
00:10:14,458 --> 00:10:16,883
Make every failure replayable.

157
00:10:17,183 --> 00:10:20,658
Find the first stage where the
evidence disappears.

158
00:10:20,958 --> 00:10:24,808
Fix the biggest bucket, and gate
every future change on it.

159
00:10:25,817 --> 00:10:29,192
The full method, with every
source, is on vidyal.ai.

160
00:10:30,217 --> 00:10:31,992
Thanks for watching.

161
00:10:32,292 --> 00:10:36,442
Next in the series, how to chunk legal
contracts for retrieval.

162
00:10:36,742 --> 00:10:38,867
Subscribe so you don't miss it.
